WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.036
For normal application queries, LINQ and provider-supported parameterized patterns are the safer default.

2
00:00:07.036 --> 00:00:18.026
EF Core can translate expressions and bound values into database operations while keeping variable values conceptually separate from the database command structure.

3
00:00:18.026 --> 00:00:23.655
This reduces the risk created by manually assembling query text from request values.

4
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:28.815
Safe APIs are strongest when query structure remains under developer control.

5
00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:36.119
A caller may provide a customer ID, a tenant ID, an order status, a date range, or a supported filter option.

6
00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:41.212
The application should decide how those values are represented in the query.

7
00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:47.980
The caller should not provide arbitrary field names, expressions, sort fragments, or query fragments.

8
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.655
Validation still matters.

9
00:00:49.655 --> 00:00:59.975
IDs, statuses, ranges, enum-like values, page sizes, and filter choices should be checked for the current business purpose before they influence behavior.

10
00:00:59.975 --> 00:01:03.258
A parameterized value can still be inappropriate.

11
00:01:03.258 --> 00:01:12.841
For example, a valid status string may not be valid for the current workflow, and a valid tenant identifier may not belong to the current user.

12
00:01:12.841 --> 00:01:16.996
Projection and data shaping are security and privacy controls.

13
00:01:16.996 --> 00:01:28.052
Returning full entities from a query can expose fields the caller does not need, such as audit values, internal flags, relationship details, or operational metadata.

14
00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:35.156
Use query shapes and response models that return only the records and fields the caller is allowed to see.

15
00:01:35.156 --> 00:01:38.975
Performance-driven query changes deserve security review.

16
00:01:38.975 --> 00:01:47.754
Optimizing a slow path may introduce broader includes, raw SQL, caching, denormalized projections, or different filtering behavior.

17
00:01:47.754 --> 00:01:57.135
These changes can be appropriate, but they should preserve authorization, tenant boundaries, data minimization, and safe parameter handling.

18
00:01:57.135 --> 00:02:00.084
Consistency makes query safety maintainable.

19
00:02:00.084 --> 00:02:06.584
Teams should document how to express common filters, sorts, projections, paging, and date ranges.

20
00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:15.831
When safe patterns are familiar and easy to find, developers are less likely to invent one-off query construction under schedule pressure.
